t some snappy  transitions or especially on rolls and  custom inversions you'll really start to  notice that the banking is uneven that's  not really the case here  but you can really see it kind of jag on  some curves and transitions here but  that aside it's really about as smooth  as I ever go on a coaster in planet  coaster the method itself basically  means that you have to build your entire  coaster out of four meter long pieces  and then afterwards smooth those pieces  out back and forth throughout the entire  track eight times and I know that sounds  very weird basically means you're going  to select four pieces at a time  hit smooth go to the next four pieces as  then move up one piece forward smooth  again move up one piece forward smooth  again then do the same thing backwards  through the entire track and repeat that  entire process four times and it's  pretty tiring but with pretty awesome  results as well so yeah that's basically  what this is I'll explain a bit more  about the method in the planet coaster  college video but I hope that this was a  pretty good intro as to how it looks and  why the result is so awesome  now without further ado let's get into  the time lapse and show you how the rest  of this is done now unfortunately I  don't really have much time lapse of me  doing the layouts at all since I really  just built the layout and Oscar which  realized that I had a go of mine  so I didn't record anything else which  was quite stupid only a little bit of  smoothing of the first drop over here  but you can kind of see me going back  and forth selecting four pieces at a  time and then humans move button the  entire time this is basically a process  that I had to go through for the entire  track though but the rest of this is  actually getting realistic touches in  all kinds of other ways a lot of my  videos so far have been a lot about  scenery and I think as a result it's  pretty easy to get the picture that  scenery is really my main stick when it  comes to planning coaster but I love  roller coasters as well and a quantum  enthusiast when it comes to following  all the latest news and watching every  single POV of every single roller  coaster on earth ever so I really wanted  to miss a bit more with that as well so  aside from just doing the  lay out as realistically as possible  here also try and experiment a lot with  making all kinds of other elements on  the coarser more realistic and one of  those was supports I wanted to get some  custom supports in here to make it look  much more be enemy and that also had to  come with some custom footers as well  for the supports to be in and that's a  lot of the stuff that I'm doing in here  one of the things that I really wanted  to get in which you can see over here  are those typical bnm little supports  which don't have one support going  straight down from the track and another  slanted support to support that but  instead the sort of triangular diagonal  sort of supports that make up this  interesting looking skyline of the  little and at the same time allow the  track to go underneath the little quite  a bit and it's really giving that very  typical bnm look that I was going for  with this coaster and also what I wanted  to add here is that typical being on  lift Hill motor and little chain return  so you've got the chain going up  underneath the track or on top of the  track actually underneath the cars and  actually hooking up the cars to go to  the top of the little but bnm courses  also have this separate chain lift  return which is the big beam that you  see going underneath the chain lift or  even underneath the entire LIXIL which  is where the chain goes back to the  beginning and allows you to pull up the  chain again only problem I kind of  realized was that I made my quick little  custom little motor there not to realize  that there is actually an in-game little  motor so I probably shouldn't have done  that that was a little bit of waste of 