ferent connectors and  that's something that I wanted to add to  the coaster as well to add a bit more  realism to it actually didn't add the  connectors yet so the yellow parts are  in there yet but they'll be there at  some point in time  also as another part of realism I wanted  to add a transfer track and a storage  strength for the coaster so at least  we'll be able to run two trains and if  there is any issue with maintenance or  anything related we can work one train  up to the storage shed or of course if  we're on a less busy day and we only  need to run one train and we're pretty  much still running fine with just that  train we can just store the other train  and save some money or not having to run  the other one so that's something that I  also wanted to quickly make over here of  course we don't quite have actual  functional transfer tracks which it's  all something that I would love to see  in one day wink-wink nudge-nudge but  it's still something that you can really  well make with all of the new metal  pieces especially given the fact that we  can recolor them now which is really  awesome and actually look really great  if you give them colors that are  slightly lighter than the colors of the  3d eyepieces since they do have a  slightly darker texture but it just  pretty much fits their color scheme as  well so I always quite happy with how  that turned out  and in this case I think I used some of  the pillar pieces to get a slightly  concrete looking footer and then we've  got some other eye pieces to make a  connector for that for the supports so  it all looks pretty good and I'm quite  happy with how that worked out  multiple not least I wanted to make a  quick little one I'm not sure what you  would call it actually but basically the  little electrical box where you actually  use the transit direct where one  employee is going to have to stand and  hit buttons and have the track switch so  you can move one train into the storage  that's basically what we're getting into  so basically all the mechanics of the  transfer track and also that I wanted to  move into some of the more aesthetic  points of the coaster which means  actually making a little shed and at  some later stage in this video actually  making a station for the coaster but  first off we've got the shed and to be  very honest as you might be able to tell  from what I'm doing here I'm really  focusing more on a realistic look and a  very sort of Six Flags Cedar Point  typical sort of amusement park but not  quite theme park look where everything  isn't so extremely detailed but it's all  very rough gritty industrial woods just  like the coaster and there's some basic  attention being drawn to landscaping and  this kind of scenery around that but  there's not really a theme to the  coaster it's not heavily themed at all  it's just put out there on a grassy  plain there's a simple park running by  and there are some basic stuff that you  need to have around your coaster put  that aside it's because that's really  the thing that it's all about so it's  it's very simple and it's got a sort of  style to it because I did want to choose  my colors and textures for these  buildings so I'm going with a very rough  gritty industrial look which means that  I'm getting some of the natural stones  out with some very sort of washed out  beige colors for a natural sandstone  sort of look getting some of the iron  roofs to get some corrugated walls in  there which works quite well with the  dark grey sort of iron beams that make  the sides and the highlights on this  building and even some yellow stripes  because it's it's sort of like  construction work it all feels like it's  in construction and sort of rough around  the edges and I really felt that that  sort of edginess was something that I  might as well add some yellow and black  stripes for as well because that just  all adds to the sense of danger and  the sense of it all just being a bit  mechanical and industrial maybe it's a  bit over the top but I still felt like  it was quite funny also something that  if you've ever played rollercoaster  country you might remember station  covers were actually a very big part of  scenery in there and there were hundreds  and I'm probably not even exaggerating  there hundreds of different types of  station covers that people designed and  a lot of them featured the yellow black  stripe sort of design which was quite  funny so definitely a shout out to the  roller coaster in three days with those  and also what I wanted to work on of  course was the cue slowed it on the  short side but I didn't want to go too  far with it and do kind of imagine this  coaster could be somewhere in a small  park and we do have a cattle pen over  here to haul in a few people but even  for the cue the rest of it is really  just going to be simple a simple metal  fence with some colours of the coaster  around it to sort of clear that it's  part of the coaster and kind of make it  a little bit more bright and nice even  though it's just metal and yeah that's  basically what's going on with the  Whitacre here it's very simple and very  straight to the point what I did try to  do though is to make it as clean as  possible so the challenge here was not  so much in trying to add as much detail  as possible or going for some sort of  more difficult atmosphere but really in  getting everything to connect nicely and  in a very clean way that nothing is  sticking out and everything just feels  very I don't know it feels very not so  message thing is if you're working with  a theme and you can just scatter barrels  around and have all kinds of different  senses have war sticking out in places  and have very these immunized buildings  and a sort of messy overall atmosphere  with many themes that can work but when  you're going for a very clean-cut simple  straight to the point industrial theme  it's something that I want to try to  avoid as much as possible 